{
  "name": "Ocean Swell",
  "created": "2026-09-13T18:00:00.000Z",
  "notes": "Long swells rolling along the strip with a shorter chop riding on them and foam catching the light. Two `wave` emitters at different wavelengths travel in opposite directions, each scrolled by a translate on a full unipolar ramp so the scroll never has a seam; where they cross, the water heaves.\n\nThe chop is a `ripple` warp on the long swell, its amount breathing on a slow sine. A perlin LFO drives the main scroll's rate, so sets of waves surge and ease rather than arriving like a conveyor, and a very slow sine stretches and compresses the wavelength itself. Foam is a fine, fast noise whose palette is black until its highest crests, where it turns near-white.",
